Here's an example JavaScript function that takes an array of five numbers and checks for valid prime numbers within the given array:

```javascript
function checkPrimeNumbers(arr) {
  // Validate input: Ensure arr has exactly 5 elements
  if (arr.length!== 5) {
    console.error("Invalid input: Array must have 5 elements.");
    return;
  }

  for (let I = 0; I < arr.length; i++) {
    const n = arr[i];

    // Check if the current number is prime using Sieve of Eratosthenes
    let flag = true;
    if (n === 1 || n <= 3) { // Special case for smallest primes - 1 & 3
      continue;
    }
    for (let j = 2; j * j <= n; j++) {
      if (n % j == 0) {
        flag = false;
        break;
      }
    }

    // Print the prime number if found
    if (flag && n > 0) {
      console.log(`Found Prime Number: ${n}`);
      break; // Found a prime, no need to check further in this iteration
    }
  } 
}

// Example usage:
const myArray = [7, 23, 12, 89, 65];
checkPrimeNumbers(myArray);
```

This function first checks whether the input `arr` has exactly 5 elements or not. Then, it loops through each number in the array, checks whether it's greater than zero (as `0` and negative integers aren't considered prime), and applies a simple implementation of the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm on the current element.

If a prime number is found during the loop, we log it to the console and stop checking further iterations since only one prime needs to be detected. Note that this implementation uses time complexity O(n log(log(n))), which is efficient for relatively small arrays like our input. For much larger inputs, more advanced algorithms may perform better.

