In TypeScript, you can specify union types to represent values that can potentially hold various data types. To further narrow down a potential union type into one specific expected type while keeping type checking, you can use what's called a **type assertion** or a **type narrowing technique**.

Here's an example:

Let's say we have a variable `myValue` that can technically accept two different types (`string` and `number`) as its value:

```typescript
let myValue: string | number;
```

Now, if you're certain about the actual runtime type of `myValue`, regardless of its possible options defined by its type annotation, you can perform type assertions to narrow its type.

#### Using Type Assertions

Type assertions allow you to explicitly convert a value to a different type, even if it wasn't originally declared with that type. Here is how you'd assert that `myValue` will always be of type `string`:

```typescript
if (typeof myValue ==='string') {
  const strVal = myValue as string; // force'strVal' to be of type string
}

// Usage inside the block
console.log(strVal.toLowerCase()); // No errors
```

Similarly, here's how you would assert `myValue` to be a number:

```typescript
if (typeof myValue === 'number') {
  const numVal = myValue as number; // force 'numVal' to be of type number
}

// Usage inside the block
console.log(Math.floor(numVal)); // No errors
```

Just remember that when using type assertions, TypeScript won't enforce any checks at compile-time, assuming you've made the correct assertion.

#### Using Type Narrowing Techniques

To maintain stronger type safety, you can also leverage control structures like conditionals, loops, or even array methods combined with pattern matching (using destructuring, type guards etc.) to achieve similar results. 

For instance, let's refine our previous example using `typeof` type guard:

```typescript
if (typeof myValue ==='string') {
  const strVal: string = myValue;
  console.log(strVal.toLowerCase());
}
else {
  const numVal: number = Number(myValue);
  console.log(Math.round(numVal));
}
```

This approach narrows the type based on your checked conditions, making them more secure since TypeScript will verify that you handle each potential type accordingly.

Either way, type assertions or type narrowing techniques help you make specific assumptions about the type of values during runtime, while still maintaining strong typechecking throughout your codebase. Just ensure you're confident enough in your understanding of the behavior for better development experience.

Here's an example showing how to generate a Table of Contents (TOC) automatically in LaTeX using the `tocloft` package, which provides advanced customization options for the TOC.

First, let's assume you have the following simple structure and content for your LaTeX file:

```latex
% Your main.tex file

\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[titles]{tocloft} % include 'tocloft' package for TOC customizations

\begin{document}

\tableofcontents % Print the Table of Contents

\newpage

\section{Introduction}
This is section 1.

\subsection{Section 1.1}
Text for subsection 1.1.

\section{Chapter Two}\label{sec:chapter_two}
This is section 2.

\subsection{Section 2.1}
Text for subsection 2.1.

\subsubsection{Sub-subsection 2.1.1}
More text for sub-subsection 2.1.1.

\section{Conclusion}
This concludes the document.

\end{document}
```

To create an automatic Table of Contents with customized styles using `tocloft`, follow these steps:

1. First, add `\usepackage[titles]{tocloft}` instead of just `\usepackage{tocloft}` at the beginning of your main `.tex` file (after your document class declaration). The `titles` option allows you to redefine headings like \section so that they behave nicely in the TOC.

2. Add `\setlength{\cftchapnumwidth}{2em}` below your preamble if you want to adjust the width of chapter numbers in the TOC (replace `2em` with desired length).

3. Before printing the actual TOC, you need to run the appropriate LaTeX commands. To tell LaTeX to create the TOC entries, you use `\markright` command before each sectioning command (like `\section`, `\subsection`, etc.). In this example, we also label the second-level section (`\section{Chapter Two}`) with `\label`.

4. Finally, add `\tableofcontents` anywhere you'd like the TOC to appear in your document body, before the start of any content (in this case, on the very first line).

After these modifications, your updated LaTeX code should look like this:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[titles]{tocloft} % Include 'tocloft' package

%\renewcommand{\cftchappagefont}{\small} % Optional: Change font size for chapter titles in TOC

\setlength{\cftchapnumwidth}{2em} % Adjust width of chapter numeral

\begin{document}

\tableofcontents 

\clearpage % Force page break after TOC

\section{Introduction}
\markright{Introduction}

This is section 1.

\subsection{Section 1.1}
\markright{Introduction\\Section~1.1}
Text for subsection 1.1.

\section{\protect\texorpdfstring{\label{sec:chapter_two}}{Chapter Two}}
\markright{Chapter Two}
This is section 2.

\subsection{Section 2.1}
\markright{Chapter Two\\Section~2.1}
Text for subsection 2.1.

\subsubsection{Sub-subsection 2.1.1}
\markright{Chapter Two\\Section~2.1\\Subsection~2.1.1}
More text for sub-subsection 2.1.1.

\section{Conclusion}
\markright{Conclusion}
This concludes the document.

\end{document}
```

When you now compile your.tex file, it will generate a properly formatted TOC based on your content.

Note: Remember to recompile your latex document multiple times until the changes propagate correctly. This is usually achieved by running `pdflatex mydocument.tex` two or more times, where "mydocument" is replaced by the name of your main LaTeX file.

To draw a Chess board based on a given FEN (Forsyth-Edwards Notation) position in LaTeX, we'll need to use some additional packages along with plain LaTeX.

1. First, install the `skak` package by adding `\usepackage{skak}` in your `.tex` file preamble:

   ```latex
   \documentclass{article}
   \usepackage[utf8]{inputenc} % Use UTF-8 encoding if needed
   \usepackage[T1]{fontenc}

   \usepackage{skak}% Package for chess boards
   % Additional packages may be needed depending on your document class
   \begin{document}
   ```

2. Now that the necessary package is included, let's create a macro (`\chessBoard`) where we can pass the desired FEN as an argument:

   ```latex
   \newcommand{\chessBoard}[1]{
     \ChessBoard[#1]
   }
   ```

3. To incorporate the FEN notation, you'll need to update your `\chessBoard` command as shown below:

    ```
    \def\BoardPosition#1,#2 #{%
      \setchessboard{#1}{#2}}

    \def\FENtoBoards#1 {\expandafter\FENboard\expandafter{#1}\end}

    \def\FENboard#1 #2\end {%
      \ifx\relax#2\relax
        \@nameuse{BoardPosition}#1
      \else
        \FENboard #2 \end
      \fi
    }

    \newcommand{\getChosenPieceColor}[1]{\IfMaterial{#1}{\White}{\Black}}

    \newcommand{\chessBoard}[1]{
      \singlespace
      \begin{tabular}{|*6{|>{\centering\arraybackslash}p{0.5cm}}|}
        \hline
        \multicolumn{7}{|c|}{} \\
        \cline{2-7}
        \multicolumn{1}{|c|}{}
        & \multicolumn{2}{c|}{\textcolor{\getChosenPieceColor{1}}{\ding{56}}} 
        & \multicolumn{2}{c|}{\textcolor{\getChosenPieceColor{1}}{\ding{57}}} 
        & \multicolumn{2}{c|}{\textcolor{\getChosenPieceColor{1}}{\ding{58}}} \\
        \cline{2-7}
        && 1 && 2 && 3 \\
        \cline{2-7}
        \FENtoBoards{#1}
        \cline{2-7}
        \multicolumn{1}{|c|}{4}
        & \multicolumn{2}{c|}{\textcolor{\getChosenPieceColor{1}}{\ding{59}}}
        & \multicolumn{2}{c|}{\textcolor{\getChosenPieceColor{1}}{\ding{52}}}
        & \multicolumn{2}{c|}{\textcolor{\getChosenPieceColor{1}}{\ding{53}}} \\
        \cline{2-7}
        && 4 && 5 && 6 \\
        \cline{2-7}
        \multicolumn{7}{|c|}{} \\
        \hline
      \end{tabular}
    }
    ```

    Here, we define several macros:

    - `\BoardPosition`: This defines the positions of pieces on each square for creating individual rows of the board using `skak`. This uses two arguments for representing ranks (rows) and columns (files).
    - `\FENtoBoards`: Converts the FEN string into LaTeX-specific row-by-row representation.
    - `\FENboard`: A recursive function called from `\FENtoBoards`, parses the FEN input, separating them into their corresponding rank and piece details.

4. Finally, include your FEN notated game and call the `\chessBoard` macro like so:

   ```latex
   \chessBoard{rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1}
   ```

This will produce a standard initial chess board with white as turn.

